    "period": "Check date 3 April 2028 (18 months after the 3 October 2026 item), with earlier disclosure possible in Nvidia's quarterly or annual filings.",
    "opens_as": "binding_obligation",
    "settled_by": "Nvidia's definitive agreement or SEC filing (10-Q/10-K) disclosing the finalized residual-value guarantee amount and terms for its AI infrastructure financing platform."
  },
  "article": {
    "publication_date": "2026-10-03",
    "title": "Amazon Pursues $8 Billion Sale-and-Leaseback of Nvidia Chips",
    "text": "Amazon Pursues $8 Billion Sale-and-Leaseback of Nvidia Chips\nAmazon is pursuing a sale-leaseback structure for approximately $8 billion worth of Nvidia AI chips, transferring them to outside investors while continuing to use them. The company plans to raise funds through bond issuance via a special purpose vehicle, with Amazon retaining use of the chips. The move is seen as a strategy to relieve AI investment burdens from its balance sheet amid capital expenditures that have ballooned to $220 billion this year. As the AI industry increasingly treats semiconductors as financial assets, the deal\u2014if completed\u2014could trigger similar asset securitization moves by other Big Tech companies.\nAmazon is pursuing a sale-leaseback structure for approximately $8 billion worth of Nvidia artificial intelligence chips, transferring them to outside investors while continuing to lease and use them. The strategy aims to relieve the surging burden of AI infrastructure investment from its balance sheet while maintaining data center computing capacity.\nAccording to the Financial Times, Amazon has recently been in contact with investors to discuss transferring approximately $8 billion worth of Nvidia \"Grace Blackwell\" chips deployed in U.S. data centers to a special purpose vehicle (SPV). The assets include thousands of AI chips installed across more than a dozen data centers in five U.S. states, including Nevada and Virginia.\nThe structure works as follows: Amazon transfers the chips it has secured to an SPV, which then issues bonds to raise capital from outside investors. Amazon subsequently leases the chips back and continues to use them in its existing data centers. This allows the company to reduce the burden of carrying expensive AI chips directly on its books while maintaining the computing capacity it needs.\nAmazon is reportedly discussing offering investors up to 10% equity in the vehicle while retaining no equity stake itself. Bond investors are expecting investment-grade ratings based on Amazon's double-A credit rating rather than the residual value of the hardware. This is a key factor enabling participation from conservative institutional capital such as insurers and pension funds.\nIf the deal closes, Amazon would be able to offload approximately $8 billion in asset exposure while maintaining use of its AI chips. Big Tech faces a dilemma: it cannot halt investment in the AI race, but carrying the full cost on its balance sheet is burdensome\u2014and that tension is now driving new financial engineering.\nAstronomical Investment Costs Spur Financial Innovation\nThe backdrop to Amazon's resort to this financing method is the astronomical growth in AI investment costs. Amazon's projected capital expenditures for this year stand at approximately $220 billion, up about 10% from the previous forecast of $200 billion. The surge is driven by aggressive investment in data centers and AI infrastructure, centered on its cloud business, Amazon Web Services (AWS).\nThe primary driver of the upward revision is rising memory prices for AI servers. Demand continues to outstrip supply. AWS's backlog reached $496 billion, posting triple-digit year-over-year growth.\nAndy Jassy, Amazon's CEO, said in July that even with significantly increased investment, the company would struggle to meet all AI computing demand.\nAt the time, he stated: \"We won't have enough capacity to satisfy all the demand we have in 2026, and the same will be true in 2027.\" He added that \"the demand already secured for 2028 is extraordinary.\" The sale-leaseback structure is seen as a compromise to address the demand pressure he referenced while preserving financial soundness.\nAcross the AI industry, there is a growing movement to treat semiconductors as a form of financial asset. Companies are borrowing against expensive GPUs and data centers as collateral, or outside investors are purchasing assets that Big Tech then leases on a long-term basis. Nvidia is also building out a chip-based financing market, partnering with Wall Street financial firms to create large-scale AI infrastructure financing platforms.\nIn August, Nvidia announced plans with six financial partners\u2014Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs, and KKR\u2014to build a platform that would attract more than $500 billion in third-party capital. Nvidia also proposed sharing up to $125 billion in risk by guaranteeing hardware residual values.\nRecent major financing deals using similar structures include:\nPartnership with 6 financial firms\nAmazon's move is part of a broader trend of converting AI infrastructure into financial products. The structure\u2014where physical assets like semiconductors and data centers serve as underlying assets for bond issuance\u2014resembles past aircraft leasing or real estate securitization, but carries differentiated risk given the rapid pace of technological obsolescence in AI chips.\nAs Nvidia's chip generation cycles accelerate, there are concerns that the value of assets held by SPVs could decline faster than expected.\nGrace Blackwell is Nvidia's cutting-edge chip, but the next-generation \"Vera Rubin\" is slated to succeed it. Amazon tells investors that each generation of semiconductors has a useful life of at least five years. Starting in 2025, the company also shortened the useful life of certain server and networking equipment from six years to five years. However, the structure\u2014in which Amazon guarantees long-term use as lessee\u2014provides investors with a degree of cash flow stability.\nMarket observers believe that if this deal closes, other Big Tech companies are likely to adopt similar approaches. With AI infrastructure investment now measured in the hundreds of billions of dollars, asset securitization offers a practical alternative for improving capital efficiency.\nAmazon has not issued an official statement regarding the specific terms of the deal or the list of investors. However, industry observers interpret the structure as a strategic choice by Big Tech to simultaneously achieve two goals: expanding AI investment and maintaining financial soundness."
  },
